raye-tpm
========

Keeps the TPM opening the encrypted disk by itself when the computer
switches between Ubuntu's kernel and the Raye kernel, and when a Secure
Boot database update (db, dbx, KEK) is written. Installed as
/usr/libexec/raye-tpm by raye-system. Python 3, standard library only;
it calls tpm2_pcrread (only when /sys/class/tpm/tpm0/pcr-*/7 cannot be
read), cryptsetup and clevis.

Why it is needed
----------------
The disk's clevis tpm2 bindings are sealed to PCR 7. PCR 7 records the
Secure Boot variables and one "authority" event per certificate that
verified a boot binary. Shim logs the certificate it used from its own
lists: Ubuntu's kernel is verified with shim's built-in certificate,
already logged for GRUB, so it adds nothing; the Raye kernel is verified
with the Raye boot key from MokList, which adds an event. A dbx update
changes the dbx event. Either way the old binding no longer opens the
disk and the password is asked.

How it works
------------
1. Read the firmware event log
   (/sys/kernel/security/tpm0/binary_bios_measurements, crypto-agile or
   SHA-1 only) and replay PCR 7 in the bank the disk uses (sha256, else
   sha1). If the replay is not the TPM's value: stop, change nothing.
2. Predict PCR 7 for the next boot:
   - the Secure Boot variable events (SecureBoot, PK, KEK, db, dbx, dbt,
     dbr) are rebuilt from /sys/firmware/efi/efivars, hashed the way this
     firmware hashed them (whole UEFI_VARIABLE_DATA or value only, found
     from the event itself; anything else: stop);
   - the Raye kernel's authority event is removed, then added at the end
     for "--kernel raye" when Secure Boot and shim's checks are on;
   - every other event is kept as recorded.
3. Bind each TPM disk to that value: `clevis luks bind -y -k - ... tpm2
   '{"pcr_bank":B,"pcr_ids":"7","pcr_digest":D}'`, D being the raw PCR
   value in unpadded base64url (what clevis decodes with `jose b64 dec`).
   The existing key comes from `clevis luks pass` on a binding that
   opens the disk now and goes to clevis on a pipe, never to a file.
4. Record the binding's role in /var/lib/raye-tpm/bindings.json
   (predicted, ubuntu-kernel, raye-kernel) with its bank and PCR 7
   value. No secret is ever written there.

Updates it cannot predict
-------------------------
fwupd writes db or dbx while it installs, after Raye Update has asked
raye-tpm to get ready, and a new shim or GRUB can be signed or carry an
SBAT level raye-tpm cannot know in advance. For those, raye-tpm
suspends the TPM protection for one start, the way BitLocker does:

1. `suspend` adds a clevis tpm2 binding with no PCR policy, keyed like
   the others, records it as "suspended" and leaves the flag
   /var/lib/raye-tpm/suspended.
2. The next start opens the disk with it, or with a binding predicted
   after the variables were written (Raye Update calls
   prepare-firmware again once fwupd is done).
3. raye-tpm-check.service runs `resume` at that start (only when the
   flag is there). If a PCR 7 binding opened the disk, the suspension
   is removed. If not, the disk is bound to the PCR 7 the TPM shows
   now, the new binding is checked to open the disk, and then the
   suspension is removed. The other installed kernel gets its
   prediction for the new values.
4. A suspension is never kept: if it cannot be resumed, it is tried
   once more at the next start, then removed anyway and the disk asks
   its password (Raye Settings offers to bind it again).

The password and recovery key slots are never touched: raye-tpm only
adds and removes clevis tpm2 bindings. resume never acts on the start
that made the suspension, so it lasts until the computer restarts.

raye-tpm-watch.path runs "prepare-firmware --if-changed" when dbx, db,
KEK or PK is written by any other tool (fwupdmgr by hand, for example).
The path unit also fires when a write is turned down by the firmware:
sbkeysync tries the same dbx update at every start on some machines.
--if-changed first compares the variables with what this boot measured
(the event log) and does nothing when they are the same, so no binding
without PCR 7 is made for an update that never happened. When the log
cannot tell, it suspends as before.

suspend and the bindings read the disk's slots under the state lock:
resume may remove a suspension at the same moment, at start-up.

The Raye kernel's event
-----------------------
Shim 15.8 logs a MokList certificate as an EV_EFI_VARIABLE_AUTHORITY
event named "MokListRT" (shim's GUID) whose value is the certificate's
MokList entry: owner GUID (16 bytes) then the DER certificate. raye-tpm
builds it from /usr/share/raye/kernel/raye-boot.der and the owner found
in the MokListRT variable (shim's GUID when it is not there yet, which
is what mokutil uses). The first time the Raye kernel really runs, the
bytes it logged are kept in /var/lib/raye-tpm/raye-authority.bin and win
from then on.

Commands
--------
  replay [--bank B]         event log -> PCR 7, compared with the TPM
  predict --kernel K        print the predicted PCR 7 ("bank hex")
  bind-next --kernel K      bind every TPM disk for the next boot of K
  prepare-firmware          suspend for one start, and bind for every
                            installed kernel from the current Secure
                            Boot variables (before and after db/dbx/KEK
                            are written); --if-changed: only when they
                            differ from what this boot measured
  suspend                   let the TPM open the disk without PCR 7 for
                            the next start only
  resume                    on the start after: seal to PCR 7 again and
                            remove the suspension
  record                    tag the bindings that open the disk on this
                            boot; count the boots the others missed
  status [--json]           per disk: which bindings open it now, roles
  prune                     remove bindings that missed 3 boots and that
                            no installed kernel can open any more

K is raye or ubuntu. A binding that opens the disk now, a binding raye-tpm
has never seen open the disk, and the last binding of a disk are never
removed; without a prediction it can trust, prune removes nothing.

Exit codes: 0 done, 1 error, 2 refused (nothing changed), 3 nothing to do
here (no TPM, no event log, no TPM disk, nothing suspended), 4 a disk
could not be bound, suspended or pruned. Messages are one English line;
the words users see are Raye Settings'.

Tests
-----
  python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests

tests/fixtures/ holds three real boots recorded by tests/make-fixtures.sh
(OVMF with Secure Boot and the Microsoft keys, a software TPM with the
sha1 and sha256 banks, the signed shim and GRUB, kernel 7.0.0-42):

  stock  the kernel as signed by its vendor
  mok    the same kernel signed by a throwaway key enrolled in MokList
         (mok-cert.der), the way the Raye kernel is verified
  dbx    "stock" after a hash was appended to dbx

Each has the event log, the Secure Boot variables and the PCR 7 the TPM
showed. The tests predict one boot from another (stock -> mok, mok ->
stock, stock -> dbx) and must get the value the TPM really showed.
